is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Impact: Desktops (192.168.200.0/24) cannot reach the laptop VLANs 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24. They can still reach other “laptops” on 172.16.10.0/24, so the break is specific to the 172.18.5x networks.

Mechanism: Traffic from desktops transits router4 -> router3 -> router6 to reach 172.18.50/51. On router6, the firewall (iptables) drops all forwarded packets arriving on the uplink (eth2, 10.50.1.2). Evidence: pc11 → 172.18.50.14 times out, while pc11 → 172.16.10.12/13 succeeds. Router4 traceroute to 172.18.50.14 reaches 172.25.1.2 (r3) then 10.50.1.2 (r6) and dies there. Router6 can locally ping 172.18.50.14 (OK), and router4 can ping 172.18.50.254 (r6 LAN IP, OK), proving the laptop VLANs and routing are up
[FINAL] stop=no-more-tool-calls tool_calls=34
[SUBMITTED] parsed_ok=True is_anomaly=True faulty_devices=['router6']
[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Impact: Desktops (192.168.200.0/24) cannot reach the laptop VLANs 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24. They can still reach other “laptops” on 172.16.10.0/24, so the break is specific to the 172.18.5x networks.

Mechanism: Traffic from desktops transits router4 -> router3 -> router6 to reach 172.18.50/51. On router6, the firewall (iptables) drops all forwarded packets arriving on the uplink (eth2, 10.50.1.2). Evidence: pc11 → 172.18.50.14 times out, while pc11 → 172.16.10.12/13 succeeds. Router4 traceroute to 172.18.50.14 reaches 172.25.1.2 (r3) then 10.50.1.2 (r6) and dies there. Router6 can locally ping 172.18.50.14 (OK), and router4 can ping 172.18.50.254 (r6 LAN IP, OK), proving the laptop VLANs and routing are up. On router6: iptables shows FORWARD policy ACCEPT but explicit rules: “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP”, “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP”, and “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP”, with only a narrow exception for TCP to 172.18.51.1:80. This blocks all forwarded ICMP/TCP/UDP arriving from the core.

Localization: Router6, FORWARD chain of iptables; traffic ingress on eth2 (10.50.1.2) towards eth0/eth1 (172.18.50/51).

Root cause: A recent firewall change on router6 added blanket DROP rules on FORWARD for interface eth2, unintentionally blocking inter-VLAN traffic from the desktop side.

Corrective fix: On router6, remove or narrow these DROP rules and allow forwarding from eth2 to eth0/eth1 (e.g., insert “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -o eth0 -j ACCEPT” and “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -o eth1 -j ACCEPT” before any drops, or flush FORWARD rules if no firewall is required). Verify with pings from a desktop to 172.18.50/51 hosts.
=== END ===